# Planning with Files

This project tracks multi-step work in three files at the project root.
Read them before major decisions, update them after major actions.

| File | Purpose | Update when |
|---|---|---|
| `task_plan.md` | Phases, status, decisions | After each phase completes or scope changes |
| `findings.md` | Research, root causes, discoveries | After ANY discovery worth remembering |
| `progress.md` | Session log, what was tried, results | Throughout the session |

## Rules

1. **Read before deciding.** Before starting a new phase or making an
   architectural call, skim `task_plan.md` (current phase) and the tail of
   `progress.md` (recent actions) — don't rely on conversation memory alone
   for long-running work.
2. **Log every discovery.** Root causes, surprising results, and decisions
   belong in `findings.md`, not just in chat, so they survive a new session.
3. **Update after acting.** After edits/commands that complete a step,
   append to `progress.md`; if a phase finished, flip its status in
   `task_plan.md`.
4. **Never repeat a failed approach.** If `progress.md`/`findings.md` show an
   approach already failed, mutate the approach — don't retry it verbatim.
5. **3-strike protocol.** Attempt 1: diagnose & fix. Attempt 2: a genuinely
   different approach (not a variant of attempt 1). Attempt 3: question
   assumptions, search for alternatives. After 3 failures on the same
   problem: stop and ask the user.
6. **Skip this for simple tasks.** Single-file edits, quick lookups, and
   straightforward questions don't need plan-file overhead.

## Why this rule exists

Cursor's `beforeSubmitPrompt` hook cannot inject context (only Claude Code's
`UserPromptSubmit` can); a `sessionStart` hook
(`.cursor/hooks/session_start.py`) surfaces `task_plan.md` + recent
`progress.md` once per new conversation, and a `postToolUse` hook
(`.cursor/hooks/post_edit_reminder.py`) reminds to update `progress.md`
after file edits. This always-applied rule is the per-message fallback that
keeps the workflow in view between those two hook firings.
